<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Legnica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Liegnitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Legnica (German Liegnitz) is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. Near it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> defeated Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> in 1241. From 1248 it was the capital of a Piast duchy whose dukes ruled until the last of the line, George William, died in 1675. Later Prussian and German, it became part of Poland in 1945 and housed the headquarters of Soviet forces in Poland until 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/legnica.jpg" alt="Legnica" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marek Śliwecki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Legnica_-_Stary_Ratusz_na_legnickim_rynku_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
